<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Merchant of Venice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1596–<a href="/years/1598">1598</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> is a play by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>, written in the late 1590s. It tells of the Venetian merchant Antonio, who borrows money from the Jewish moneylender Shylock on the bond of a pound of his flesh, and of Portia, who saves him in court. Its portrayal of Shylock has made it one of Shakespeare's most discussed plays. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> translated it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-merchant-of-venice.jpg" alt="The Merchant of Venice" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merchant_venice_tp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The play is believed to have been written between 1596 and 1598. Francis Meres mentioned it in 1598, and its first edition of 1600 says it had been performed divers times. A reference to the ship the Andrew is thought to allude to the Spanish ship St. Andrew, captured by the English at Cádiz in 1596. James Roberts entered it in the Register of the Stationers Company on 22 July 1598, and Thomas Heyes published the first quarto in 1600. That edition is regarded as accurate and is the basis of the text in the 1623 First Folio.</p><p>A merchant forfeiting a deadly bond after standing surety for a friend was a common tale in late 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The test of the suitors, the rescue from the pound of flesh by the friend's new wife disguised as a lawyer, and her demand for a betrothal ring appear in Il Pecorone by Giovanni Fiorentino, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1558. The story of the three caskets is in the Gesta Romanorum, probably compiled at the end of the 13th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Bassanio, a young Venetian who has squandered his estate, wishes to woo Portia, the wealthy heiress of Belmont, and needs 3,000 ducats. His friend Antonio, a merchant, has no ready cash because his ships are at sea, so Bassanio turns to the Jewish moneylender Shylock, and Antonio guarantees the loan. Though the First Folio classes the play as a comedy, it is remembered mostly for its dramatic scenes and for Shylock's demand for a pound of flesh. It holds two well-known speeches: Shylock's Hath not a Jew eyes? and Portia's on the quality of mercy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Critics disagree over whether the play is antisemitic. Harold Bloom argued in 1998 that it is deeply so, and others point to Shylock's plea for the rights of all who suffer discrimination. English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had been expelled under Edward I in 1290 and could not return until 1656, and in Venice Jews had to live in a ghetto and wear a coloured hat in public.</p><p>The earliest recorded performance was at the court of King James in the spring of 1605. In 1701 George Granville's adaptation, The Jew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, played the role of Shylock comically and was acted for about forty years. Charles Macklin returned to the original text in 1741. According to the actor Jacob Adler, the tradition of a sympathetic Shylock began with Edmund Kean in the first half of the 19th century, and Henry Irving's proud Shylock of 1879 was called the summit of his career. Adler himself played the part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Operas based on the play include Reynaldo Hahn's Le marchand de Venise, first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 25 March 1935.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Merchant of Venice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Julius Nyerere translated Shakespeare’s Julius Caesar and <b>The Merchant of Venice</b> into Swahili, part of his effort to make Swahili a language of national culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Nyerere, the first president of Tanzania, translated Shakespeare’s Julius Caesar and <b>The Merchant of Venice</b> into Swahili, to show that the language could carry any literature in the world.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
